\subsection{Leave-One-Environment-Out Cross-Validation}
\label{sec:nve_loeo}

To assess whether $\theta{=}1.5\times$ generalizes beyond the in-sample evaluation, we perform leave-one-environment-out (LOEO) cross-validation across Walker ($n{=}15$), Cheetah ($n{=}5$), and PickCube ($n{=}2$). For each fold, we hold out all experiments from one environment, select the optimal accuracy-maximizing threshold range on the remaining training set, and evaluate the fixed $\theta{=}1.5\times$ on the held-out environment.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Leave-one-environment-out cross-validation of NVE threshold $\theta=1.5\times$.
For each fold, the threshold is validated on an environment whose data was \emph{not} used
to select $\theta$. The optimal threshold range on each training set is reported;
$\theta=1.5$ falls within the optimal range in all three folds.}
\label{tab:nve_loeo}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Held-out & $N_{\text{train}}$ & $N_{\text{test}}$ & $\theta_{\text{opt}}$ range & Test Accuracy & Test AUROC \\
\midrule
PickCube & 20 & 2 & $(1.45,\, 1.53]$ & 2/2 (1.000) & 1.000 \\
Cheetah  & 17 & 5 & $(1.45,\, 1.53]$ & 5/5 (1.000) & 1.000 \\
Walker   &  7 & 15 & $(1.00,\, 3.78)$ & 14/15 (0.933) & 0.963 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Mean} & — & — & — & \textbf{0.978} & \textbf{0.988} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The threshold $\theta{=}1.5\times$ falls within the optimal accuracy range for all three training folds: $(1.45, 1.53]$ when Walker data is available, and the wider $(1.00, 3.78)$ when training on Cheetah$+$PickCube only (where CIVO and non-CIVO NVE values are well-separated). The single cross-validation error is exp\_008e\_v3 seed~2 (Walker), an IRD collapse mechanistically distinct from CIVO (\S\ref{sec:roc_analysis}). Mean held-out accuracy is 0.978 and mean AUROC is 0.988, confirming that $\theta{=}1.5\times$ generalizes across environments without retuning.
PickCube fold confidence intervals remain wide (Clopper-Pearson 95\% CI: $[0.158, 1.000]$) due to $N_{\text{test}}{=}2$.

% --- APPENDIX B: Factorial Ablation Details ---

\section{Factorial Ablation Details}
\label{sec:appendix_factorial}

This appendix provides the full factorial ablation results summarized in \S\ref{sec:f3f5_summary} of the main text.

\paragraph{Embedding gradient analysis.}
Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb} isolates embedding \emph{trainability} as the necessary condition for CIVO by comparing four gradient conditions under otherwise identical configurations ($H{=}8$, subsample ON, $\dt{=}2$ imagination).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{$\dt$-embedding gradient conditions on Walker Walk. Any gradient pathway updating $e_{\dt}$ is sufficient to induce CIVO; freezing or removing the embedding dramatically reduces severity.}
\label{tab:three_way_emb}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lccccl}
\toprule
Condition & Grad flow & Exp(s) & NVE peak & Eval & CIVO \\
\midrule
Trained & all & 008d, 059 & $1.53$--$4.72\times$ & 64--268 & 5/5 \\
Reward detach & obs+KL only & 044 & $4.45\times$ & 44$\to$538$^\dagger$ & 1/1 \\
Full SGS & KL only & 018 & $1.66\times$ & 448 & 1/1$^*$ \\
Frozen & none & 042, 015b, 047, 057 & $0.19$--$1.88\times$$^\ddagger$ & 204--612 & 2/4 \\
Removed & N/A & 008f & $0.97$--$1.41\times$ & 307--503 & 0/3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
{\raggedright\footnotesize $^*$Full-SGS seed~1 (NVE $1.66\times$); seed~2 collapsed. $^\dagger$Self-corrected at ${\sim}175$K (eval 44$\to$538, 59\% baseline; 224K run). $^\ddagger$Exp\_047 (NVE $1.71\times$) and Exp\_057 (NVE $1.88\times$): both transient, self-correcting within 20K steps.\par}
\end{table}

The reward-detach condition (exp\_044, \texttt{reward\_dt\_emb\_detach=True}) uses a backward hook to block reward-head gradients from reaching $e_{\dt}$, complementing the framework-level Reward SGS (exp\_016e). Both yield the same directional result---CIVO onset not prevented---ruling out implementation artifacts. Exp\_044 exhibits delayed CIVO onset (${\sim}95$K vs.\ typical $25$--$35$K), consistent with reduced but non-zero gradient pressure on $e_{\dt}$. Over a full 224K run, exp\_044 self-corrects: eval recovers from 44 (95K) to 538 (215K, 59\% baseline), but this recovery is incomplete compared to spontaneous recovery without gradient intervention (exp\_045: 868, 95\% baseline). The lower asymptote suggests that selectively disrupting gradient flow may create optimization difficulties beyond CIVO itself.
